## Authoring Ergonomics Issues (2026-05-03)

Identified by reviewing all demos and documentation. Numbered for tracking. None yet started.

### Lexer / Parser

- [ ] **AE-1 — Apostrophes in prose text.** Any contraction or possessive (`it's`, `player's`,
  `you'd`) breaks the lexer: `'` starts a symbol literal token. Confirmed in demo21 (had to
  rewrite "wayfarer's" to "wayfarer inn").
  **Decision: change the symbol literal sigil from `'` to backtick (`` ` ``).** Backtick is
  completely free in the current lexer, never appears in English prose, and the fix is
  mechanical: one branch in `compiler/lexer.lua`, plus search-replace across all `.sb` files
  and `idea.md`. Context-sensitive apostrophe detection was considered but rejected — it
  adds ongoing lexer complexity and still breaks leading-apostrophe cases.
  Affects: `compiler/lexer.lua`, all `.sb` demo/test files, `idea.md`.

- [ ] **AE-2 — UTF-8 characters silently corrupted in prose.** Em-dashes (`—`), arrows (`→`),
  curly quotes, ellipsis (`…`) are skipped or produce `ILLEGAL_CHAR`. Confirmed in demo21.
  **Fix is ~6 lines in `compiler/lexer.lua` only — no parser changes needed.** The `> 127`
  branch currently calls `adv()` (silently drops the byte). Change it to: decode the full
  UTF-8 sequence length from the leading byte (1–4 bytes), call `adv(seq_len)`, and emit a
  new `RAW_TEXT` token with the multi-byte string as its value. The parser's
  `parse_narration_text` (parser.lua:2186) already handles arbitrary token kinds via its
  catch-all else branch (`tok_display_and_len → tostring(v), #v`), so `RAW_TEXT` tokens
  slot in without any parser edits. String literals already round-trip UTF-8 correctly.
  Affects: `compiler/lexer.lua` only.

- [ ] **AE-5 — No escape for literal `{` in narration.** `{expr}` is an inline interpolation;
  there is no way to display a literal brace. Any game with rules text or code-like content
  is blocked. Fix: add `\{` or `{{` as an escape sequence. Affects: `compiler/lexer.lua`,
  `compiler/parser.lua`.

- [ ] **AE-6 — Narration lines starting with a keyword produce confusing parse errors.**
  The parser tries to parse any scene body line as a statement first. A line like
  `Set the scene here.` tries to parse `set` as a keyword. Fix: improve the error message
  to suggest the line might be intended as narration. Affects: `compiler/parser.lua`.

### Runtime / Semantics

- [ ] **AE-3 — Engine time axis and state variable are independent and must be kept in sync
  manually.** `time-inc! day: 1` advances the engine time axis (used by `query-at`,
  `query-history`). `inc! world/day 1` advances a game state variable. They are completely
  independent; a demo author must do both. Fix candidates: (a) a `time-axis: world/day`
  annotation that makes `time-inc!` also advance the state variable, or (b) a
  `time-model: day-var: world/day` unification field. Affects: `runtime/eval.lua`,
  `compiler/parser.lua`, `idea.md`.

- [ ] **AE-4 — `query-at` returns nil for paths that were never mutated, even if they have
  a declared initial value.** `init_defaults` does not emit log entries, so
  `query-at player/gold time: {day: 0}` returns nil when gold was initialized to 100 and
  never changed on day 0. Authors expect the initial value to be queryable. This forced
  demo21's third verify block to be reworded. Fix: emit a log entry for each state path
  at engine startup time (seq=0, time=initial time), before any player action.
  Affects: `runtime/engine.lua` (`init_defaults` or `init`).

- [ ] **AE-8 — `verify` `after (fn):` always starts from a fresh initial state, not from
  arbitrary reachable states.** Authors cannot write "after buying from any state where we
  have enough gold, gold decreases." The `from-any-state:` + `requires:` workaround is
  more complex. Fix: document this clearly as a known limitation in `docs/reference/language.md`,
  and consider whether `after (fn):` should optionally sample from BFS-explored states.

- [ ] **AE-12 — No error when `=>` subscene forgets `<-`.** The scene stack grows
  unboundedly until it hits `scene-stack-max` and crashes with a generic overflow error.
  Fix: at overflow, report the full scene stack so the author can identify which subscene
  is missing its `<-` exit.  Affects: `runtime/engine.lua`.

### Missing Features

- [ ] **AE-7 — Actor `perceives:` must be kept in sync manually.** Every path an actor reads
  must appear in `perceives:`. Adding a read without updating `perceives:` is a compile error.
  During iteration this is constant friction. Fix: add a `perceives: *` wildcard escape hatch
  for dev mode, or auto-infer `perceives` from the behavior function's static read-set
  (the checker already computes write-sets; read-sets are symmetric). Affects:
  `compiler/checker.lua`, `runtime/actors.lua`.

- [ ] **AE-9 — No debug print / trace from `.sb` code.** The only way to observe intermediate
  values during development is to `set! player/description (str "debug: " val)`. Fix: add a
  `trace expr` builtin that writes to stderr in dev mode and is stripped in production builds
  (tagged `debug-only`). Affects: `runtime/eval.lua`, `compiler/codegen.lua`.

### Documentation Gaps

- [ ] **AE-10 — Return value semantics for multi-statement pure functions undocumented.**
  Single-expression pure functions auto-return (spec §7.1 examples). Multi-statement pure
  functions use explicit `return` (demo15). The rule is not stated: does the last expression
  auto-return, or is `return` required? Clarify in `docs/reference/language.md` §7.

- [ ] **AE-11 — Multi-segment path access on loop variables undocumented.** `{entry/time/day}`
  in a `for` loop body works because eval checks whether the first path segment is a
  `ctx.vars` key and then navigates table fields. This is a critical feature for rendering
  log entries but appears in no documentation. Add an explicit example to
  `docs/reference/language.md` in the `for` loop and temporal query sections.
